I'm here in Bahrain with vice admiral  Brad Cooper the commander of U.S naval  forces in the Middle East and we are  here to talk about cutting-edge drone  technology and the future of the Navy so  thank you so much for your time here  today great to be here I'd like to start  with this concept of the Navy's future  Fleet what does that look like and what  percentage of the fleet is manned and  what percentage is unmanned the chief of  Naval operations Emma White gilday has  his laid out division that the future  Fleet is a combination of manned and  unmanned there are a large number of  efforts going on throughout the Navy to  achieve this Vision we're one of them  our Leading Edge effort is focused on  taking unmanned surface vessels and  integrating those with artificial  intelligence to put more eyes out on the  water which expands our knowledge of  what's Happening that contributes to  Greater deterrence greater vigilance and  greater Regional security which is good  for everybody and of course it's not  just about these individual vessels it  is about what the Navy calls digital  ocean can you explain that yeah in  simple terms the digital ocean is a  synthesis of all the sensors that we can  pull together from seabed to space  sensors that are on the water under the  water in the sky bringing them all  together to enhance our understanding of  what's happening on above and underneath  the sea so what sort of capabilities  does a digital ocean give the US Navy  today we integrate manned systems with  these new unmanned systems and the  technology and what does that give us it  allows us to see further than we've been  able to see in the past you couple that  with artificial intelligence and you now  give yourself the opportunity in a very  large theater to detect things that a  human couldn't detect and then bring  that information back into a command  center so that a human can make a  decision big picture though it allows us  to increase our deterrence and our  understanding what's happening around  the theater so that we can use our man  vessels more effectively and you are are  rapidly testing and integrating them but  you've also been using some of these  unmanned vessels in the past few months  can you tell us what you have learned  from those vessels yeah we're doing both  of those so we are definitely  experimenting taking a Cutting Edge  technology bringing it from around the  world and bringing it here as we're  doing today and then we're integrating  with artificial intelligence and then  we're also operating it in on the  operational side we do manned and  unmanned teaming where we take these new  platforms and we team with a man vessel  to get the most out of what the sensors  can provide we're very happy with where  we are in the direction we're headed  there one event that made a lot of news  was Iran tried to seize two of these  drones that you've been using as you put  more unmanned vessels in the water does  the potential for conflict increase yeah  I think it's first important to note  that from our interaction with Iran that  their actions were flagrant they were  unwarranted they certainly weren't the  actions that we'd expect with a  professional Naval force and most  importantly their actions were a  violation of international law beyond  that we have not changed our operating  patterns the United States Navy and our  partners fly and sail with international  law allows and that's what we'll  continue to do in the future how is the  Navy preparing for and thinking about  the proliferation of these unmanned  vessels not just Americas but  competitors and allies around the world  well right now we're on the Leading Edge  of it and our goal is to stay on the  Leading Edge how close behind are other  countries China Russia Iran yeah I would  I wouldn't try to gauge you know how  close they are we're definitely leading  in this space and we're particularly  leading with the ability to integrate  not just the platforms but with  artificial intelligence and I'll tell  you the secret sauce that allows us to  do that is the people that we that we  have the people or that are allowing us  both success at the beginning  reassessment very quick adjustment and  be able to bring things to a closure in  a very effective way so that's really  how we're going to maintain the  advantage it's my understanding too that  task force 59 has inspired other task  forces throughout the military to do  similar work with sensors and  surveillance reconnaissance how  ultimately will these task forces and  these efforts integrate together yeah  I'll just use the example here in U.S  Central Command where we have all the  service components so task force 59 is  the Navy component the Air Force's ninth  Air Force now has a task force 99 the  U.S Army's third Army has task force 39.  so these three components will work  together under a common vision from the  centcom commander to advance our  Innovation and that helps the Navy and  the US military broadly see ocean floor  to space absolutely seabed to space  integrating sensors in the most dynamic  vibrant way that we can so none of the  platforms that are being used here or  experimented with here are armed is that  correct we're focused on domain  awareness right now that's correct but  as the war in Ukraine has showed us  drones armed drones in the sky and in  the water will inevitably play a very  big role in the future of warfare how is  the Navy how is the military preparing  for armed vessels armed systems in the  water yeah I think we all recognize that  the arm systems in the air have been  around for 20 to 30 years at this point  underneath the sea you have sensors that  have operated unmanned undersea vehicles  for about 10 years and so we're on the  Leading Edge of this work on the surface  right now we're focused on the domain  awareness and understanding of what's  happening and I'm certain in the future  we'll look to advance the capability can  you tell me more about the importance of  Israel's participation in your efforts  yeah a couple things about Israel number  one they're an extraordinary capable  Navy and Military so having them in a  partner adds value you to Maritime  security particularly in the Red Sea and  that's good for everybody more  specifically both the Abraham Accords  and their transition in alignment from  U.S European command to your Central  Command has allowed us to work more  closely than we've ever been able to  work before so in the last year just  from a bilateral basis we've done more  than 30 exercises with Israel enhancing  our overall proficiency interoperability  and then more broadly because they are  quite proficient at Sea and in  multi-domains and in unmanned they bring  extra value to the sensors that are  participating in this exercise and again  I think at the end of the day you'll  find their participation helps ensure  increase in deterrence and greater  Regional stability that's good for the  free flow of Commerce and that's good  for everybody in the region  you're building out a fleet of 100  unmanned platforms by the end of next  summer that will be an international  Fleet is that correct yeah the goal is  as you mentioned 100 unmanned surface  vessels patrolling the waters around the  Middle East by the end of next summer  the United States would have about 20 of  those but our partner nations would  resource the remaining 80 and we're on  path to do that what we do offer now is  the latest and greatest that's out there  both in unmanned systems and artificial  intelligence Admiral Cooper thank you so  much thanks great to chat with you  appreciate it it's great to be here
 

